 ## Overview
-The Per-Gaussian Backward Pass is a parallelization strategy for the backward pass of 3D Gaussian Splatting that computes gradients by iterating over Gaussians rather than pixels [S1, unofficial]. It solves the high atomic contention and false sharing inherent in standard pixel-parallel backpropagation [S1, unofficial]. By reducing the number of required atomic operations by a factor equal to the number of pixels in a tile (typically 256 for 16×16 tiles), it significantly accelerates training time at the cost of increased VRAM usage [S1, unofficial].
